{
  "kind": "tea",
  "teaId": "jael-tent",
  "text": "Fair warning. This one is violent, and I am not softening it, because Scripture doesn't.\n\nSisera. Commander of nine hundred iron chariots. Twenty years of crushing Israel. And he has just watched his entire army fall apart, so he's running. On foot. Alone.\n\nHe heads for the tent of a woman named Jael, because there's peace between his king and her husband's house. He thinks he's found neutral ground.\n\nJael comes out to meet him. And she says, \"Turn in, my lord, turn in to me. Fear not.\"\n\nFear not. Remember that.\n\nHe goes in. She covers him with a blanket. He says, \"Give me a little water to drink, for I am thirsty.\" She opens a skin of milk instead, gives him a drink, and covers him again.\n\nThen he gives her instructions. Stand in the door. If anybody asks whether there's a man in here, say no.\n\nAnd the text says he was fast asleep, and weary.\n\nSo Jael takes a tent peg and a hammer, and goes softly, and it is over.\n\nThen she walks out, finds Barak, and says, \"Come, and I will shew thee the man whom thou seekest.\"\n\nThe audacity of that sentence.\n\nAnd here's the part people skip. Deborah puts it in a song. \"Blessed above women shall Jael be.\" He asked for water. She gave him milk.\n\nScripture does not flinch. And neither will I.",
